  abstract = {Steady-State Visual Evoked Potential (SSVEP) relies on chromatic contrast between stimuli and backgrounds to elicit robust neural responses. Color perception is based on three color attributes: hue, saturation, and lightness. Alterations to any of these elements change color perception. However, the systematic effects of color attributes on SSVEP performance remain underexplored, particularly in complex visual environments (e.g., extended reality). This study systematically examines how controlled variations in hue, saturation, and lightness affect SSVEP signal-to-noise ratios (SNRs) across six background colors, while also evaluating perceptual sensitivity through subjective visibility ratings (invisible, barely visible, and visible). Eighteen healthy participants performed an SSVEP task with stimuli flickering at 10 Hz, systematically modulated in hue$(\pm 5^\circ$to$\left.\pm 25^\circ\right)$, saturation$(\pm 5 \%$to$\pm 25 \%)$, and lightness ($-5 \%$to$-50 \%$) against varied backgrounds. Findings revealed that greater chromatic contrast in all three attributes led to enhanced SSVEP SNRs. Stimuli presented on different background colors yielded varying SNRs, with red backgrounds eliciting the strongest responses. Notably, even stimuli rated as invisible or barely visible elicited detectable SSVEP responses, suggesting the feasibility of using low-contrast stimuli in practical applications. These findings provide a foundation for designing SSVEP systems that balance s},
}

