  abstract = {Afferent and efferent visual dysfunction are prominent features of multiple sclerosis (MS). Visual outcomes have been shown to be robust biomarkers of the overall disease state. Unfortunately, precise measurement of afferent and efferent function is typically limited to tertiary care facilities, which have the equipment and analytical capacity to make these measurements, and even then, only a few centers can accurately quantify both afferent and efferent dysfunction. These measurements are currently unavailable in acute care facilities (ER, hospital floors). We aimed to develop a moving multifocal steady-state visual evoked potential (mfSSVEP) stimulus to simultaneously assess afferent and efferent dysfunction in MS for application on a mobile platform. The brain-computer interface (BCI) platform consists of a head-mounted virtual-reality headset with electroencephalogram (EEG) and electrooculogram (EOG) sensors. To evaluate the platform, we recruited consecutive patients who met the 2017 MS McDonald diagnostic criteria and healthy controls for a pilot cross-sectional study. Nine MS patients (mean age 32.7 years, SD 4.33) and ten healthy controls (24.9 years, SD 7.2) completed the research protocol. The afferent measures based on mfSSVEPs showed a significant difference between the groups (signal-to-noise ratio of mfSSVEPs for controls: 2.50 ± 0.72 vs. MS: 2.04 ± 0.47) after controlling for age (p = 0.049). In addition, the moving stimulus successfully induced smooth pursuit },
